**Read:** [Cryogenic Engineering — M. D. Atrey, IIT Bombay](../sources/Cryogenic-Engineering-Atrey.lingua#r_65F71B6VYE8297NF8ZN1D3S783), liquefaction and refrigeration; [Turbines, compressors, valves, mixers and nozzles](../../../physics/thermodynamics-and-heat-transfer/balances/Turbines-compressors-valves-mixers-and-nozzles.lingua#r_5FNNDS9HS3F0FDBD0MX1H5D8YJ) and real-fluid states.

For a steady adiabatic valve with negligible work and kinetic/potential changes, inlet and outlet specific enthalpies are equal. This does not mean temperature is constant. The Joule–Thomson coefficient μJT=(∂T/∂p)h determines the local temperature change and depends on the fluid state. An ideal gas with enthalpy depending only on temperature has μJT=0.

A work-producing expansion can reduce enthalpy and follows a different account. Compare ideal isentropic and actual turbine performance using the same inlet and outlet conditions. Two-phase formation may limit equipment choices. A gas that cools in one temperature range need not cool in another.

**Work:** Derive the valve and turbine energy statements. Use a documented real-fluid model or table to compare two inlet states under throttling. Track phase appearance and explain the role of precooling in a liquefaction cycle. Verify that a claimed temperature drop is consistent with the energy boundary.

**Return:** [Industrial gases, vacuum and high-purity supplies](../Industrial-gases-and-vacuum.lingua#r_3GHQJEYMYEN6JDKSF4PPJ403RF).
